# Alt text

Alternative text, or alt text, describes an image in words. Screen readers read it out. Browsers show it when the image does not load, such as on a wall panel with a weak Wi-Fi signal, or when someone has turned images off.

## Every image needs an alt attribute

Give every image an `alt` attribute, even if you leave it empty, `alt=""`. An empty alt tells a screen reader to skip the image.

Of the picture cards, only the Picture card lets you set alt text, with the `alt_text` option. In a Markdown card, you can write an `<img>` tag with its own `alt`.

```yaml
type: picture
image: /local/garden-plan.png
alt_text: Back garden. The shed is at the far end, with the water butt on its left.
```

Writing `alt_text: ""` does not give the image an empty alt. If the card shows an entity’s image, Home Assistant uses the entity name instead. If not, it leaves out the `alt` attribute altogether. If a picture is only decoration, take it off the dashboard.

Other cards do not let you set alt text:

| Card | What Home Assistant uses as alt text |
| --- | --- |
| Picture entity | The entity ID, such as `camera.front_door` |
| Picture glance and Picture elements | The entity ID if the card has an entity, otherwise an empty alt |
| Area card picture | An empty alt, so screen readers skip it |
| Tile card with an entity picture | An empty alt, so screen readers skip it |

On these cards, describe the image in text next to it, such as in the card name or a Markdown card under it.

## When to use alt text

Add alt text only to describe what the image shows that you cannot practically say in the text around it.

Leave the alt empty, `alt=""`, when:

- the image is decorative and holds nothing people need
- the image is an icon with a visible label, so alt text would repeat the label
- the image is inside a link, and the link text already says where it goes

Write alt text for a floor plan or photograph that gives information. Describe what it shows that people need.

## Writing good alt text

Good alt text:

- tells people what information the image gives them
- describes what is in the image and what it is for
- is specific, meaningful and short

Use normal punctuation, such as commas and full stops, so it is easy to read and to hear. Aim for no more than 2 sentences.

Do not write alt text that:

- names the person who took the photograph or made the image
- starts with “Image of”, “Photo of” or anything else that would be the same for every image
- repeats what the card or page already says
- adds information that is not in the image

[Images](../content/formatting-content/images.html) in the writing guide explains how to word alt text, with before and after examples.

### Example: a photograph of the stopcock

A household puts a photograph of their stopcock beside the leak sensor card, so anyone at home can turn off the water quickly. The card under it already says “Kitchen leak sensor”.

```yaml
type: picture
image: /local/stopcock.jpg
alt_text: The stopcock is at the back of the cupboard under the kitchen sink, to the left of the waste pipe. It has a red handle.
```

This alt text says where the stopcock is and how to recognise it, in 2 sentences. These would be worse:

| Alt text | Problem |
| --- | --- |
| Image of stopcock | Starts with “Image of”, and does not say where it is |
| Photo by Sam, March 2026 | Names who took it, and says nothing about what it shows |
| Kitchen leak sensor | Repeats the card name |
| Stopcock under the sink. Turn it off if the leak sensor detects water. | Adds an instruction that is not in the photograph. Put it in text on the dashboard. |

### Example: an illustration of pairing a door sensor

On a dashboard, a Markdown card beside this illustration lists the 3 steps, as the text under it does here. The alt text does not repeat the steps. It says what only the picture shows: what the button looks like and where the light is.

<%= colour_example('images-pairing-steps-alt', callout: false) %>

```yaml
type: picture
image: /local/door-sensor-pairing.svg
alt_text: The pairing button is the round white button on the front of the door sensor, with a small light above it.
```
